WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.033
Eliminating buffer overflows in Rust is a
layered engineering practice.

2
00:00:04.113 --> 00:00:07.531
Start with safe Rust abstractions: arrays,
vectors, slices, strings,

3
00:00:07.611 --> 00:00:10.688
byte slices, checked access, iterators,
and pattern matching.

4
00:00:10.768 --> 00:00:14.699
These tools carry length and validity
information that raw buffer handling often

5
00:00:14.779 --> 00:00:15.121
loses.

6
00:00:15.201 --> 00:00:16.853
Treat parsing as a trust boundary.

7
00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:21.036
Validate input length, encoding,
delimiters, record structure, supported values,

8
00:00:21.116 --> 00:00:24.420
and version expectations before data
drives indexing, allocation,

9
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:27.349
file paths, database operations, or
downstream requests.

10
00:00:27.429 --> 00:00:31.247
Convert raw input into validated domain
types where that makes the application

11
00:00:31.327 --> 00:00:32.466
easier to reason about.

12
00:00:32.546 --> 00:00:33.629
Control resource use.

13
00:00:33.709 --> 00:00:37.412
Buffer overflow prevention is not enough
if a safe program can still allocate

14
00:00:37.492 --> 00:00:40.284
without limit or spend unbounded time on
malformed input.

15
00:00:40.364 --> 00:00:43.441
Review length and capacity assumptions,
integer conversions,

16
00:00:43.521 --> 00:00:45.857
maximum sizes, timeouts, and failure
behavior.

17
00:00:45.937 --> 00:00:48.957
Keep unsafe and FFI boundaries small,
documented, and tested.

18
00:00:49.037 --> 00:00:52.569
Review pointer validity, buffer length
contracts, ownership transfer,

19
00:00:52.649 --> 00:00:56.353
allocation and deallocation
responsibility, alignment, initialization,

20
00:00:56.433 --> 00:00:57.572
and callback behavior.

21
00:00:57.652 --> 00:01:01.641
Wrap low-level behavior in safe APIs that
prevent ordinary callers from violating

22
00:01:01.721 --> 00:01:02.746
the buffer contract.

23
00:01:02.826 --> 00:01:04.934
Finally, maintain buffer safety over time.

24
00:01:05.014 --> 00:01:08.547
Test empty, short, oversized, malformed,
and invalid-encoding inputs.

25
00:01:08.627 --> 00:01:10.279
Add regression tests after fixes.

26
00:01:10.359 --> 00:01:12.467
Review dependencies and parser libraries.

27
00:01:12.547 --> 00:01:13.972
Watch operational failures.

28
00:01:14.052 --> 00:01:16.331
The goal is not just to avoid one old
class of bug,

29
00:01:16.411 --> 00:01:20.228
but to build Rust software where buffer
boundaries remain visible, validated,

30
00:01:20.308 --> 00:01:21.220
and safe by design.
